Document (dev.stxt.website): STXT vs JSON — Hand-written documents vs serialization
	Metadata:
		Author: Claude Fable 5
		Revision: Joan Costa Mombiela
		Last modif: 2026-09-08
		Description: STXT and JSON compared with examples: hand-writing, comments, multi-line text, JSON Schema versus templates, and the canonical JSON tree of every STXT document.

	Header: @STXT@ vs JSON

	Assert >>
		JSON is a serialization format for machines; @STXT@ is a markup language for people.

	Content >>
		JSON has a minimal grammar, a single data model and parsers in every language. The
		comparison is limited to the point where the two overlap —a file that a person
		writes and maintains by hand— and ends where @STXT@ adopts JSON: as the
		representation of its own tree.

	Subheader: The same content, twice

	Content >>
		An article record in JSON:

	Json >>
		{
			"article": {
				"title": "Modern Software Architecture",
				"published": "2026-09-01",
				"tags": ["software", "architecture"],
				"summary": "A survey of monoliths and microservices.\nSecond line, with \"quotes\"."
			}
		}

	Content >>
		And in @STXT@:

	Code >>
		Article: Modern Software Architecture
			Published: 2026-09-01
			Tag: software
			Tag: architecture
			Summary >>
				A survey of monoliths and microservices.
				Second line, with "quotes".

	Content >>
		The differences are those of the JSON grammar, designed for one program to emit
		and another to read:

		* **Delimiters.** Keys and strings in quotes, objects in braces, elements separated
		  by commas, and a comma after the last element is a syntax error. In @STXT@ the
		  structure is the indentation and a name ends in `:`.
		* **No comments.** The JSON grammar does not admit them. @STXT@ has `#` comments,
		  and the tools keep them when reformatting.
		* **A text is one line.** Line breaks are written `\n` and quotes are escaped. In
		  @STXT@ a `>>` block holds the literal text, with its line breaks and no escapes.
		* **No schemas in the language.** JSON Schema is a separate specification, written
		  in JSON, with its own vocabulary of composition and references.

	Subheader: JSON Schema vs a template

	Content >>
		The structure of the article, described in JSON Schema:

	Json >>
		{
			"$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
			"type": "object",
			"properties": {
				"article": {
					"type": "object",
					"properties": {
						"title": { "type": "string" },
						"published": { "type": "string", "format": "date" },
						"tags": { "type": "array", "items": { "type": "string" }, "minItems": 1 },
						"summary": { "type": "string" }
					},
					"required": ["title", "published", "tags"],
					"additionalProperties": false
				}
			},
			"required": ["article"],
			"additionalProperties": false
		}

	Content >>
		And the same structure as an @STXT@ template, which reads like the document it
		describes:

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.articles
			Structure >>
				Article (com.example.articles):
					Published: (1) DATE
					Tag: (+)
					Summary: (?) TEXT

	Content >>
		Two underlying differences. The @STXT@ content model is closed by definition: an
		undeclared child is an error, with no need for `additionalProperties: false` at
		every level. And `DATE` is always checked, whereas in JSON Schema `format` is by
		default an annotation, which a validator may not check unless asked to. In
		return, JSON Schema expresses more: patterns, numeric ranges, conditionals and
		composition (`oneOf`, `$ref`), which the @STXT@ schema layer leaves out on purpose
		([STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC §11](stxt-schema-ref#s11)). The article, with a namespace:

	Code >>
		Article (com.example.articles): Modern Software Architecture
			Published: 2026-09-01
			Tag: software
			Tag: architecture
			Summary >>
				A survey of monoliths and microservices.

	Subheader: From @STXT@ to JSON

	Content >>
		@STXT@ does not compete with JSON as an interchange format: it adopts it.
		[STXT-TREE-SPEC](stxt-tree-ref) defines the canonical JSON representation of the
		tree of every valid document, identical across implementations. The
		`stxt describe` command prints it, and the three libraries expose it in their
		API. The article above, in that form:

	Json >>
		[
			{
				"name": "Article",
				"canonicalName": "article",
				"namespace": "com.example.articles",
				"form": "inline",
				"value": "Modern Software Architecture",
				"children": [
					{
						"name": "Published",
						"canonicalName": "published",
						"namespace": "com.example.articles",
						"form": "inline",
						"value": "2026-09-01",
						"children": []
					},
					{
						"name": "Tag",
						"canonicalName": "tag",
						"namespace": "com.example.articles",
						"form": "inline",
						"value": "software",
						"children": []
					},
					{
						"name": "Tag",
						"canonicalName": "tag",
						"namespace": "com.example.articles",
						"form": "inline",
						"value": "architecture",
						"children": []
					},
					{
						"name": "Summary",
						"canonicalName": "summary",
						"namespace": "com.example.articles",
						"form": "block",
						"lines": ["A survey of monoliths and microservices."]
					}
				]
			}
		]

	Content >>
		It is longer than the hand-written JSON because it is not the same data: it is the
		complete tree, with the name as written, its canonical form and the namespace of
		every node, which is why any program that reads JSON can consume it without an
		@STXT@ parser. The source stays readable; the serialization is mechanical.

	Subheader: When to use each

	Content >>
		JSON, for what machines exchange: API payloads, serialized state, generated
		artifacts that nobody edits.

		@STXT@, when there is a person at either end of the file: documents,
		[configuration](use-cases-config-files),
		[corporate records](use-cases-corporate-docs),
		[content that feeds a pipeline](use-cases-cms), with the canonical tree wherever
		JSON is expected. The [AI and LLMs](ai-llms) use case walks that route:
		generated documents, validated and consumed as JSON. The short version of this
		page is in the [FAQ](faq#why-not-json).
